{"created": "2026-07-19T12:11:38.468883+00:00", "modified": "2026-07-19T12:11:40.932005+00:00", "id": 21441082, "conceptrecid": "21441081", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21441082", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.21441081", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21441082", "metadata": {"title": "An Inverse-Square Law from a Future-Phase-Position Acceleration Map and Harmonic Closure in a Closed Two-Body AB Phase System", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21441082", "publication_date": "2026-07-19", "description": "This preprint reanalyzes a previously published closed two-body AB phase-system experiment in which a nonzero acceleration-like second-difference structure emerged from the unlabeled relation f_AB without introducing an external force law, background spacetime, gravitational equation, or Coulomb equation. The earlier sweep varied the initial positional-phase deviation as an amplitude while keeping the harmonic update period fixed, and therefore produced a proportional native readout rather than an inverse-square dependence. The present work interprets a future phase position on the closed orbit as a relational rotation center and uses the acceleration map alpha=R omega^2. For every nonzero integer harmonic n, the same harmonic closure fixes both the phase-cell width Delta theta_n=2 pi/|n| and the angular speed omega_n=n omega_1, giving |omega_n| Delta theta_n=Omega. Their connection yields alpha_n=R Omega^2/(Delta theta_n)^2. Under the specific conditions of the reported experiment, the inverse-square law is established. No inverse factor, area-dilution term, spherical shell, external force, or gravitational constant is inserted. The existing numerical data also verify the nonzero acceleration-like second difference and exact closure residual for all eight tested conditions. Generalization to arbitrary closed systems, harmonic arrangements, nonharmonic updates, or distance readouts remains untested; the paper does not identify the result with standard gravity.", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Kihara, Noriaki", "affiliation": "WF System Co., Ltd.", "orcid": "0009-0004-6753-4020"}], "keywords": ["closed phase system", "inverse-square law", "acceleration-like second difference", "future phase position", "harmonic closure", "phase-cell width", "two-body relation", "relational geometry"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.21318696", "relation": "isSupplementTo", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.21315735", "relation": "isSupplementTo", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.21318700", "relation": "isSupplementTo", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}], "version": "v1.0", "language": "eng", "license": "cc-by-4.0", "imprint_publisher": "Zenodo", "upload_type": "publication", "publication_type": "preprint", "prereserve_doi": {"doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21441082", "recid": 21441082}}, "title": "An Inverse-Square Law from a Future-Phase-Position Acceleration Map and Harmonic Closure in a Closed Two-Body AB Phase System", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21441082", "html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21441082", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21441082", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21441081", "badge": "https://zenodo.org/badge/doi/10.5281%2Fzenodo.21441082.svg", "conceptbadge": "https://zenodo.org/badge/doi/10.5281%2Fzenodo.21441081.svg", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21441082/files", "bucket": "https://zenodo.org/api/files/0079af32-cfda-4398-8381-a7daed018db9", "thumb250": "https://zenodo.org/record/21441082/thumb250", "thumbs": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/record/21441082/thumb10", "50": "https://zenodo.org/record/21441082/thumb50", "100": "https://zenodo.org/record/21441082/thumb100", "250": "https://zenodo.org/record/21441082/thumb250", "750": "https://zenodo.org/record/21441082/thumb750", "1200": "https://zenodo.org/record/21441082